Feature Plan: dalogin SQLAccess family split

Objective
- Decompose the 483-line `SQLAccess` class into family-scoped data-access beans that follow the
  decomposition model already proven in `mbooks-quarkus`.

Relationship to the roadmap and prior work
- Completes the structural portion of the `dalogin-datasource-pooling` split.
- Applies the same reasoning that retired `BookController` and `BookingHandlerImpl` in `mbooks-quarkus`
  (roadmap item 8, marked DONE).
- Reduces roadmap item 1 (static mutable state) and eases roadmap item 7 (gateway pattern reduction).

Role ownership
- Implementation: `@backend-dev`
- Family boundary review: `@backend-dev`
- Verification: `@testing-engineer`

Precondition
- The CDI method/resource edits must be complete earlier in the unified adaptation pass. No
  intermediate compile is required; splitting static, context-bound methods would require doing the
  injection work twice.

Implementation model
- Follow `speckit.system.dalogin-persistence-servlet-adaptation.implementation-example.md`. It is
  the single final-state model for family beans, transactions, Servlet callers, and HTTP boundaries.
  - also group new files into family-folders

Family model
- Login / registration (`AccountManager`): account lifecycle, credential hash, identity lookup, activation state, and
  registration completion. Registration and login are one family.
- Voucher (`VoucherManager`): voucher lifecycle and token copying.
- Device / session / token (`DeviceSessionManager`): device registration, token retrieval, session creation, and logout.
  Merged per `FR-017` — see the boundary decision below.
- Password reset (`PasswordResetManager`): reset token issuance and confirmation-code retrieval.
- Shared support (`SqlSupport`, static, no connection): UUID generation and SQL-error helpers, used by all families.

Boundary decisions
- Settled (`OQ-001`): `wrapUpRegistration` is a login/registration-family method, because
  registration and login are one family. It is not an orchestration service above the families.
- `copyToken2` is voucher-driven but token-shaped. Assign it by which table it owns, not by its
  name: it updates `voucher_states`, so it is voucher-family.
- Settled (`OQ-003`): the callerless `insertVoucher` and `copyToken2` wrappers are carried forward
  unchanged rather than deleted. Deletion is a behavioural subtraction this structural refactor does
  not need, and `FR-014` delegation already prevents the SQL from being written twice.
- Settled (`OQ-002`): device/token and session are one family. The reason is table ownership, not
  bean size — `insert_sessionCreated` and `logout_device` both write `device_states`, `logout_device`
  also writes `Tokens`, and `get_token2` reads `Tokens JOIN Last_seen`. A split would leave
  `Last_seen` as the only session-owned table and produce two beans mutating each other's tables.

Package placement
- Use a dedicated persistence subpackage per family rather than leaving beans in the module root,
  consistent with how `mbooks-quarkus` groups controllers, services, mappers, and records.

Implementation phases
1. Boundary agreement
   - All three open questions are settled; no boundary decision remains outstanding.
   - Mark each method that participates in another family's transaction.
   - Produce the final method-to-family assignment table.
2. Shared support extraction
   - Move `generateUUID`, `logSQLException`, `ignoreSQLException`, and `jsonSQLError` to a shared type.
   - Confirm the shared type performs no connection access.
3. Family extraction
   - Extract one family at a time, moving methods verbatim, into its own family-folder package.
   - Callers are not touched per family: each Servlet/filter/listener is edited exactly once, after
     all families exist, under the unified adaptation plan (phase 4).
   - Do not compile between families.
4. Monolith removal
   - Delete `SQLAccess` once it is empty.
   - Confirm no facade or pass-through replacement was introduced.
5. Caller tightening
   - Ensure each caller injects only the families it uses.

Validation strategy
- Extract families in increasing order of caller count so early mistakes are cheap.
- Diff moved SQL strings against the originals to prove verbatim movement.
- Compile once and run `LoginFlowTest` only after the full datasource/CDI/family/Servlet pass. It is
  the automated gate (`FR-019`); every other flow is owner-verified manually.
- Confirm the final class count and method distribution match the agreed assignment table.

Risks and mitigations
- Risk: family boundaries chosen by call site rather than domain produce a second monolith.
  - Mitigation: families are domain-named `*Manager` beans — `AccountManager`,
    `DeviceSessionManager`, `VoucherManager`, `PasswordResetManager` — each in its own
    family-folder package.
- Risk: `wrapUpRegistration` gets duplicated across families.
  - Mitigation: single documented owner is a hard requirement (`FR-005`).
- Risk: splitting `wrapUpRegistration`'s participants into other families silently breaks atomicity,
  because a plain bean call acquires a second pooled connection.
  - Mitigation: connection-passing is mandatory (`FR-011`, `FR-012`); participants never acquire,
    close, commit, or roll back, but delegeted to quarkus.
    - db owned prepared statements may be used as required in other methods, as now.
- Risk: the combined pass breaks many callers at once.
  - Mitigation: edit each caller once into its final family-bean and request/response shape; use the
    compiler after the pass as the missed-caller list.
- Risk: transaction scope changes beyond the approved login adaptation.
  - Mitigation: move SQL verbatim; only `establishLogin` may combine the former device/session/token
    calls, as required by `FR-020`.
    - db owned prepared statements may be used as required in other methods, as now.

Intent
- Forward-only. All families are extracted before any caller moves, so the pass is fixed forward
  rather than reverted per family; the only revert is the whole combined pass from git.
